=== FIDE

The #link("https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chess.pl?tid=86339")[2015 FIDE World Cup]
@fide-2015
#index[2015 FIDE World Cup]
was an S-Tier, 128-player single elimination chess tournament held at the Fairmont Hotel in Baku
from September 10th to October 5th.
#index[Fairmont Baku]
#index[Baku]

Boasting a massive #usd(1600000) prize pool, the grueling 25-day event served as a critical qualifier
for the 2016 Candidates Tournament, drawing an elite field of the world's top grandmasters, including
Veselin Topalov #index[Veselin Topalov],
Hikaru Nakamura #index[Hikaru Nakamura],
and Fabiano Caruana #index[Fabiano Caruana].

The tournament culminated in an extraordinarily dramatic, all-Russian final between
Sergey Karjakin #index[Sergey Karjakin]
and Peter Svidler #index[Peter Svidler].

After Svidler surged ahead with a 2–0 lead in the four-game classical matches, Karjakin launched a historic comeback to equalize the score.
The ensuing rapid and blitz tie-breaks devolved into a titanic, blunder-filled, and exhausted slugfest where remarkably not a single
game ended in a draw, leading to Karjakin ultimately securing a 6–4 victory to crown himself the World Cup champion.

#line(length: 100%)

The 2015 FIDE World Cup in Baku carried intense geopolitical significance as a high-profile vehicle for Azerbaijan’s
nation-building and aggressive "soft power" diplomacy, operating alongside the massive
#link("https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2015/jun/11/european-games-what-you-need-to-know-baku-2015")[2015 Baku European Games] to
project an image of a wealthy, modern, and oil-financed global sporting hub.
This @PR:a push deliberately countered intense Western scrutiny over the host country’s severe crackdowns on domestic
political dissent and human rights.
Most critically, the event served as a high-stakes flashpoint for the frozen Nagorno-Karabakh conflict.

Following years of bilateral sports boycotts—including an Armenian grandmaster previously forcing the relocation of a Candidates
tournament away from FIDE-Baku and the
#link(
  "https://en.armradio.am/2012/11/28/bakupledges-to-ensure-the-security-of-armenian-athletes-at-world-chess-cup/",
)[Azerbaijani Ministry of Youth and Sports]
had to issue unprecedented personal security guarantees to secure the participation of top Armenian grandmasters like
Levon Aronian #index[Levon Aronian].

Furthermore, the final victory of Karjakin — a prominent, Ukrainian-born grandmaster who had controversially transferred his
allegiance to
Russia #index[Russia]
#link(
  "https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/conflict-since-2001/ukraine/how-russia-annexed-crimea-in-2014",
)[following the 2014 annexation of Crimea]
@iwm-2014-russo-crimea
#index[2014 Russian Annexation of Crimea] - added a layer of post-Soviet narrative friction, cementing the tournament as a
microcosm of South Caucasus and Eastern European power politics.

Consequently, the 2015 FIDE World Cup held unique strategic weight for three of the world's most powerful nations,
serving as a soft-power arena where national sporting dominance directly intersected with broader global rivalries.

For the Kremlin, the tournament was a massive ideological triumph that reinforced Russia's
historical hegemony over international chess.
The historic, all-Russian final between Svidler and Karjakin guaranteed a Russian victory, but it was Karjakin's ultimate win
that carried the heaviest political capital.
Having recently transferred his athletic allegiance from Ukraine to Russia following the 2014 annexation of Crimea,
Karjakin -
#link(
  "https://www.insidehook.com/culture/sergey-karjakin-profile-advance-world-chess-championships",
)[a fierce, vocal nationalist who frequently wore Vladimir Putin t-shirts]
@insidehook-karjakin - became a powerful domestic symbol of Moscow's geopolitical expansion, using his World Cup victory as a springboard
to challenge for the world title.

For China, the event signaled the rapid rise of China's "Brain Power" diplomacy on
the global stage, fresh off the heels of the Chinese national team winning the
#link(
  "https://www.chess.com/news/view/first-ever-gold-for-china-at-olympiad-russia-wins-women-section-4716",
)[2014 Chess Olympiad]
@chesscom-2014-china-olympiad
and the
#link(
  "https://www.chessdom.com/china-confirms-domination-wins-world-team-championship-2015/",
)[2015 World Team Championship]
@chessdom-2015-china-world-team
.
The competition in Baku put the incredible depth of Chinese chess talent on display, most notably through
#link(
  "https://www.thetimes.com/travel/destinations/asia-travel/china/wei-16-joins-the-chess-immortals-jt8xtjr3ddc",
)[16-year old prodigy Wei Yi]
@times-wei-yi
, who narrowly missed qualifying for the Candidates Tournament after pushing Svidler to five consecutive tie-break
draws in the quarterfinals.
For Beijing, the tournament proved that China was no longer just a challenger, but an elite superpower capable of threatening
traditional Soviet-Russian dominance over the game.

For the @US:a, the 2015 FIDE World Cup was viewed as a critical proving ground for a modern "Chess Renaissance" aimed at breaking
the Russian monopoly on the sport.
The @US:a sent
#link("https://www.uschess.org/index.php/September-/-The-US-Team-at-the-World-Cup-And-Then-There-Were-Three.html")[
  an exceptionally strong, multi-generational team of nine players to Baku] -
including world-class contenders Hikaru Nakamura, Fabiano Caruana, and Wesley So, alongside a 14-year old Sam Sevian.
Though the American favorites ultimately fell short of the finals, the depth of their roster solidified the @US:a as a powerhouse,
perfectly setting the stage for the
#link("https://liquipedia.net/chess/FIDE_Candidates_Tournament/2016")[2016 Candidates Tournament in Moscow]
and eventually the #link("https://worldchess.com/nyc2016")[Championship in @NYC:a].
